// Selecting code by the version of the compiler building it.
//
// Ideally the version is read while compiling: only the taken branch reaches
// the binary, and the others are never type-checked, so they may use features
// the running compiler does not have. Jux has no such query yet (a GAP).
//
// GAP: Jux has no compiler-version query. `@cfg` knows the target, the build
// mode, the profile and the package's features (JUX-LANG-V1 §11.2), but not
// which `juxc` is running, and there is no constant carrying its version. The
// compile-time half of this lesson therefore has no Jux spelling today.
//
// What follows is the part that does exist: the version as a value, compared
// with an ordering operator, and a branch on the result. The value is a
// stand-in written by hand, and the branch runs at run time rather than while
// compiling. Where `#compiler.version` would appear, the comments say so.

// A version, compared field by field. A record gives equality and hashing for
// free; ordering is the one operator it needs to be told about, and `<=>`
// alone brings `<`, `<=`, `>` and `>=` with it (§7.14.4).
record SemanticVersion(int major, int minor, int patch) {
    public int operator<=>(SemanticVersion other) {
        if (major != other.major) {
            return major <=> other.major;
        }
        if (minor != other.minor) {
            return minor <=> other.minor;
        }
        return patch <=> other.patch;
    }
}

// Stand-in for `#compiler.version`. Jux is at 0.1, and this is the number a
// compiler query would report; it has to be kept up to date by hand, which is
// the whole problem a real query solves.
SemanticVersion compilerVersion() {
    return new SemanticVersion(0, 1, 0);
}

// The two milestones the branches are about.
SemanticVersion firstPreview() {
    return new SemanticVersion(0, 1, 0);
}

SemanticVersion firstStable() {
    return new SemanticVersion(1, 0, 0);
}

// Here the chain is an ordinary `if`, decided at run time, and the
// last branch can only report the problem after the program has been built.
void describe() {
    final SemanticVersion version = compilerVersion();
    if (version >= firstStable()) {
        print("Stable release: the 1.x language is frozen");
    } else if (version >= firstPreview()) {
        print("Preview release: conditional compilation is available");
    } else {
        print("Version requires Jux 0.1.0 or newer");
    }
}

void main() {
    final SemanticVersion version = compilerVersion();
    print($"Compiled with Jux ${version.major}.${version.minor}.${version.patch}");

    describe();

    // The nearest Jux has to "compile this only on a new enough compiler" is
    // `if cfg(...)` on something the build does know, such as a feature.
    if (version.minor >= 1) {
        print("The `if cfg` statement is supported");
    }
}
